WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.040
Everyone's favorite dog-loving killing machine
is back for his highly anticipated threequel,

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.960
and now we've been left to piece together
the puzzle of another pulse-pounding final

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:10.460
act.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.560
Here's the ending of John Wick: Chapter 3
- Parabellum, explained.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.160
Parabellum follows the satisfying formula
of the previous Wick flicks, so the amped-up

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.779
events of Act 3 don't come as a total surprise.

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:29.310
In Morocco, The Elder, the bigwig in The High
Table, a super-serious worldwide council of

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:33.489
crime lords, kicks off the film's final act
by giving Wick a choice.

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:39.070
He can kill Continental NYC manager and Wick
ally Winston and live his remaining days as

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.640
a High Table hitman.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.280
Or he can die in the Sahara.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:47.300
After a gruesome act of solidarity, slicing
off his ring finger and giving his wedding

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.480
band to The Elder, Wick heads to the Continental,
where a surprising switcheroo awaits him,

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.100
courtesy of The High Table.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.440
Every Wick fan knows that there are two rules
in the Wickverse that carry serious consequences

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.950
if they're broken: not honoring a "marker"
which is a blood-bound oath and, most importantly,

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:09.440
conducting assassination business on any Continental
property around the globe.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.640
In Chapter 3's closing act, a mysterious figure
known as The Adjudicator voids the latter

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.611
law, declaring the NYC Continental as "deconsecrated"
in order to send heavily armored High Table

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.140
thugs into the hotel.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:27.240
These super-soldiers are tasked with taking
down John Wick, since Wick chose not to take

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:31.070
down Winston, despite the whole cutting-off-his-own-ring-finger
thing.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:36.010
This armored assault doesn't work, of course,
and, after much fancily-choreographed grappling,

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:41.380
Wick defeats the High Table goons and The
Adjudicator's hired assassin, Zero, and his

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.340
blade-happy crew.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.940
After the dust settles, The Adjudicator agrees
to let Winston stay in the safety of the hotel,

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.580
but lets Winston know that Wick is still very
much a thorn in the High Table's side.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:59.080
What Winston does next is a bit ambiguous,
but it sure looks like betrayal at first glance:

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:04.520
In the swanky rooftop garden of the Continental,
last seen in Chapter 2, Winston suddenly shoots

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.840
Wick several times, causing him to fall to
the alley below.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.620
Did Winston really intend to kill Wick?

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.140
It's left unclear, but there's certainly an
argument to be made that Winston would have

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:19.760
just shot Wick in the head if he truly wanted
him dead, instead of shooting him in the bulletproof

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.420
suit he received from The Elder.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.300
Plus there's the Tick Tock Man all-too-conveniently
waiting nearby to help escort Wick's body

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.280
away, not to mention the exchange between
Continental concierge Charon and Winston where

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.160
Charon assures his boss that everything's
"in place."

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.920
Winston's plan, it appears, was to fake Wick's
death with the help of The Bowery King and

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.760
then use Wick to try and take down The High
Table.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.640
With this in mind, and Wick's fall being somewhat
slowed by awnings outside the Continental,

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.640
a building Winston knows better than anyone,
Wick's survival at the end can't just be a

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:54.660
case of good fortune.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.359
Further evidence of this risky plan comes
early, when Wick strategically shoots an underworld

00:03:00.359 --> 00:03:05.060
doctor in such a manner that it appears as
if he was trying to kill him, but he's left

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:05.560
alive.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:10.800
If Wick knows how to convincingly fake-kill
somebody, Winston probably does as well.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.040
Bottom line: If Winston wants to take down
The High Table, he knows the only man capable

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:21.140
of it is Wick, the unstoppable "boogeyman"
every trained killer knows and respects.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.200
The real question is whether or not Wick,
who genuinely seemed surprised when he was

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.720
shot by Winston, was in on it.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.440
Did Wick fake his reaction?

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:36.040
Did Wick and Winston work this all out off-screen,
during, say, the "DECONSECRATED" montage featuring

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.840
the Mad Men-meets-Suicide Girls receptionists?

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:43.300
We don't know for sure, but it's a safe bet
that Winston chose to save Wick's life by

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.920
faking his death, at least temporarily.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.340
With no evidence of his demise, Wick's in
the same place he was at the end of Chapter

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.440
2: excommunicated and wanted by every killer-for-hire
in the Wickverse.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:04:00.300
But this time, Wick appears to be done running,
and is likely eager to turn the tide against

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.440
the all-powerful High Table.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.080
As Charon puts it to Winston after Wick's
rooftop tumble: "Well played, sir."

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.980
After Winston's supposed betrayal, Wick's
fate is unknown to The High Table until The

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.860
Adjudicator sees that the super assassin's
body has gone missing.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.040
In a twist that everybody and their pitbull
saw coming, it turns out that Wick is alive,

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.300
having found refuge in The Bowery King's new
hideout.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:29.020
The Bowery King, sliced seven times by Zero,
a callback to when he hooked Wick up with

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.400
seven bullets back in Chapter 2, tells Wick
he's "pissed off."

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.310
Wick agrees with that sentiment, setting up
what's sure to be another beautifully choreographed

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.120
and absurdly violent installment in John Wick:
Chapter 4.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:46.900
Nothing's official regarding John Wick 4 yet,
but director Chad Stahelski told Entertainment

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.240
Weekly in 2018 that the Wick well hasn't run
dry.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:55.220
"If people go see the movie, and it makes
money, and they came back to us, Keanu and

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:56.640
I have ideas for days."

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.180
Interestingly enough, Stahelski later told
Indiewire that, despite all appearances, the

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.720
end of John Wick 3 wasn't necessarily intended
as a cliffhanger.

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:10.960
The trusty Wick formula, according to Stahelski,
demands that when the end credits roll, Wick

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:16.450
is either left alive and hunted by the deadliest
people on the planet, or he's dead as a doornail.

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:17.840
"There's no escape for you."

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:19.940
The director told Indiewire,

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.440
"Do you think he's going to ride off into
the f---ing sunset?

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.620
He's killed 300 f--king people and he's just
going to [walk away], everything's okay?

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.740
If you're this f---ing guy, if this guy really
exist[ed], how is this guy's day going to

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:32.240
end?

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.660
He's f---ed for the rest of his life.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.250
It's just a matter of time."

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:41.840
The somewhat ambiguous ending to Chapter 3
will likely get cleared up in Act 1 of John

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:42.320
Wick 4.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:47.100
But until then, our artisanal gold coins are
on Winston waging a "skirmish," with Wick's

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.880
help, on the seemingly invincible High Table.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.320
"You've got a nasty surprise comin'."

